     4→# Plan Review: Zen Overlay Header Redesign
     5→
     6→## Summary
     7→The plan proposes redesigning the `ZenModeOverlay` header to match the new "organic" design language. It replaces the title with source metadata, swaps the back button for a collapse chevron, and adds a "mark done" checkmark. It also aims to introduce a scroll-based transparency effect (frosted glass).
     8→
     9→## Codebase Context
    10→- **Component**: `ZenModeOverlay` inside `client/src/components/ArticleCard.jsx`.
    11→- **Current Layout**: A flexbox column layout (`flex flex-col`) where the Header, Progress Bar, and Content are sibling elements.
    12→  ```jsx
    13→  <div className="flex flex-col ...">
    14→    <div className="... header ...">...</div> (Sibling 1)
    15→    <div className="... progress ...">...</div> (Sibling 2)
    16→    <div className="overflow-y-auto ...">...</div> (Sibling 3)
    17→  </div>
    18→  ```
    19→
    20→## Critique
    21→The plan is **Approved with Modifications**.
    22→
    23→### 1. Layout Blindspot (Critical)
    24→The plan proposes changing the header's background to transparent/blurred (`bg-white/80 backdrop-blur-md` vs `bg-transparent`) to let content "scroll behind it".
    25→**However, the current DOM structure prevents this.**
    26→Because the header is a flex sibling of the content container, they do not overlap. The header sits *above* the content in the document flow. Making the header transparent will simply reveal the white background of the parent container, not the scrolled content text.
    27→
    28→**To achieve the "content scrolls behind header" effect:**
    29→1. The Header must be removed from the flow (e.g., `absolute` or `fixed`).
    30→2. The Content container must span the full height.
    31→3. The Content container needs top padding (`pt-`) so the initial text isn't hidden behind the header.
    32→
